        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "5a7d93f0da66e07afc731e20a7e884861f472b300f498ede8dee57e0d2e5e047"
    },
    {
      "type": "proposition",
      "content": "Bayes' theorem flips the conditioning variable, allowing us to move from $ \\Pr(x \\mid \\theta) $ to $ \\Pr(\\theta \\mid x) $. With $ k $ possible values of $ \\theta $ ($ \\theta_1 $ through $ \\theta_k $), the discrete version of Bayes' theorem becomes $$ \\Pr(\\theta_{j}\\mid x)=\\frac{\\Pr(x\\mid\\theta_{j})\\Pr(\\theta_{j})}{\\Pr(x)}=\\frac{\\Pr(x\\mid\\theta_{j})\\Pr(\\theta_{j})}{\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{k}\\Pr(\\theta_{i})\\Pr(x\\mid\\theta_{i})}\\quad for\\quad1\\leq j\\leq k $$",
      "source_page": 1190,
      "source_pages": [
        1190
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix2:p002:b13",
        "Evolution_appendix2:p002:b14"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          287.0,
          819.0,
          1055.0,
          869.0
        ],
        [
          350.0,
          883.0,
          931.0,
          972.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "5edd7e05ceaa517ed4c01b2901fda56c3d3373e02ead80268f2eeca6ea4ab190"
    },
    {
      "type": "discussion",
      "content": "In Bayesian statistics, x represents an observable variable (the data), while $ \\theta $ represents a parameter describing the distribution of x. In this setting, $ \\Pr(\\theta) $ is the prior distribution of possible parameter values, while $ \\Pr(\\theta \\mid x) $ is the subsequent posterior distribution of $ \\theta $ given the observed data x and the prior. In classical statistics, the unknown parameters are treated as fixed and the data are considered random, whereas under a Bayesian analysis, the data are considered fixed and the unknown parameters that generated the data are considered random.",
      "source_page": 1190,
      "source_pages": [
        1190
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix2:p002:b16"
      ],
      "source_bboxes": [
        [
          287.0,
          991.0,
          1059.0,
          1152.0
        ]
      ],
      "provenance_score": 1.0,
      "content_sha256": "40276814a8e3d7cdea38e5474d27d6ce6353bceb88d1320070be82f78b49c1ca"
    },
    {
      "type": "proposition",
      "content": "All of the above statements also hold for continuous random variables, for which the probability density function, p, replaces the discrete probability value, Pr. In particular, the continuous multivariate version of Bayes' theorem is $$ p(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}\\mid\\mathbf{x})=\\frac{p(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})p(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})}{p(\\mathbf{x})}=\\frac{p(\\mathbf{x}\\mid\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})p(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})}{\\int p(\\mathbf{x},\\boldsymbol{\\Theta})d\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}} $$ where $ \\boldsymbol{\\Theta} = (\\theta_1, \\theta_2, \\cdots, \\theta_n) $ is a vector of $ n $ (potentially) continuous variables. As with the univariate case, $ p(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}) $ is the assumed prior distribution of the unknown parameters, while $ p(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta} \\mid \\mathbf{x}) $ is the posterior distribution given the prior, $ p(\\boldsymbol{\\Theta}) $, and the data, $ \\mathbf{x} $.",
      "source_page": 1190,
      "source_pages": [
        1190
      ],
      "source_block_ids": [
        "Evolution_appendix2:p002:b17",
        "Evolution_appendix2:p002:b18",
        "Evolution_appendix2:p002:b20"
      ],
